## Summary

When a Workboard verifier checks an artifact, record which revision they checked and distinguish a current verdict from a historical or unknown one when the linked artifact record changes.

## Problem to solve

An artifact-backed verdict currently records an artifact ID, not its revision. Replacing the artifact record with v7 under the same ID preserves the old v6 “confirmed” verdict. A reader seeing that verdict and the current artifact can incorrectly conclude that v7 was checked. The original verdict is useful history and should not be removed or relabeled as a failed check.

## Proposed solution

- Keep this in the opt-in Workboard plugin. At verification time, capture a bounded revision identity for the managed artifact record. On read/export, distinguish **current**, **changed**, or **unknown** for legacy/unversioned or unavailable references. Show the distinction wherever Workboard presents that verdict.
- Preserve the original verdict, checker, scope, and timestamp. Do not change card status, completion, dependency scheduling, access, or mandatory evidence requirements. Do not fetch arbitrary URLs or imply a remote file is immutable.
- **Acceptance:** A v6 verification reads current. Editing the card title or saving the same artifact record leaves it current. Replacing that record under the same ID with v7 marks the historical verdict changed on card read and export, and in a view that presents the verdict. A fresh check of v7 creates a new current verdict; unversioned legacy verdicts read unknown. No outbound fetch occurs.

## Alternatives considered

- Ask people to remember to mint a new ID or to read the check timestamp: neither binds the verdict to the actual record it checked.
- Clear verdicts on update: loses useful history and makes unrelated edits dangerous.
- Fetch or hash every remote artifact: broadens network access and storage responsibilities, and is unnecessary for detecting a managed record replacement.

## Impact

Workboard users who rely on verification in handoffs and reviews can tell which evidence version supports the conclusion. The ambiguity occurs when an artifact-backed verdict is followed by a same-ID record replacement. This was deterministic in the component fixture; frequency in deployed boards was not measured. Cost: small additional metadata, an explicit legacy/unknown state, and tests to ensure unrelated writes do not invalidate a verdict.

## Evidence/examples

1. Create a card with inert artifact metadata: ID `poem-index`, label `poem-index.csv`, versioned URL ending in `v6.csv`.
2. Record a verdict of `confirmed` for “The poem index contains 42 entries,” linked to that ID.
3. Update the stored artifact record to `v7.csv`, keeping the same ID, and read the card back.
4. **Observed:** SQLite returns v7 as the current artifact and the unchanged `confirmed` verdict linked only to `poem-index`. The previous verdict has no field that identifies v6 or marks it stale. This is a persisted-record result, not a browser finding.

The test below uses the production Workboard store with disposable SQLite. It ran at [3f3a4b0c03b330d61b9b17d489dc2909ac572370](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/commit/3f3a4b0c03b330d61b9b17d489dc2909ac572370); the verification, write, and Gateway paths below were also compared with [main at 99da32e80e8b](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/commit/99da32e80e8b8e93c93d97865797f7768b2d2e8f) and were unchanged. Rechecked 2026-11-21: the named source files are unchanged on main at e201b64f8d7. No providers or URL fetches were involved. Same-URL remote byte replacement was not tested.

- [Verification record construction](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/3f3a4b0c03b330d61b9b17d489dc2909ac572370/extensions/workboard/src/store-verification.ts#L159-L208) stores the artifact ID; [store write](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/3f3a4b0c03b330d61b9b17d489dc2909ac572370/extensions/workboard/src/store-workflow.ts#L307-L335), [card update](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/3f3a4b0c03b330d61b9b17d489dc2909ac572370/extensions/workboard/src/gateway.ts#L461-L466), and [export](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/3f3a4b0c03b330d61b9b17d489dc2909ac572370/extensions/workboard/src/gateway.ts#L657-L660) are existing owners.
- [Benign prior art: stale report predicate](https://github.com/BaaRai/ARTEX/blob/5bed53b6ae9bf3b29e29988f43e42d5ce3870587/report/quality.go#L45-L56) distinguishes a checked version from the current version. This proposal does not import ARTEX's report or evidence store.

## Do you plan to open a PR for this?

No, proposing the idea only.

## Additional information

Follow-up to [#197450](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/197450) / [#197471](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/197471), which introduced additive verification records and kept them separate from completion. [Draft #161618](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/161618) proposes completion and handoff gates, not the revision identity of an artifact-backed verdict. [#113309](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/113309) concerns historical proof deleted under metadata pressure; this fixture shows the opposite shape: the historical verdict survives, but its subject cannot be distinguished from the current artifact.

