Closes #203061

## What Problem This Solves

`memory_search` shows only the first 700 characters of each hit, but the index stores chunks of up to 1,600 characters. A fact in the second half of a chunk is therefore invisible to anything that reads only the snippet, such as the Active Memory recall helper, which then reports nothing found or summarizes a neighboring fact.

## User Impact

User impact: a `memory_search` hit now carries its whole chunk, so recall finds facts that sit late in a chunk. Results for chunks under 700 characters are unchanged; longer chunks return up to 1,600 characters (at the default chunking), about 900 more per hit at most.

## Why This Change Was Made

The snippet limit follows the effective chunking instead of a fixed 700: the chunker's own ceiling helper, `resolveMarkdownChunkMaxChars` (the budget the chunker already uses, now exported once and re-exported from the memory-core indexing SDK surface), feeds the keyword, path and vector searches. No new setting. A chunk's text is never longer than that ceiling (the chunker checks the weighted budget, shared with the overlap, before every append), so a hit is never shorter than its chunk; the KNN worker and every other caller are untouched. Heading-only merges in index preparation can still exceed the ceiling on `main` until #201611 bounds them; this PR does not change that. Production diff: +16/-5 lines in three files (net +11: the helper, its export, and one accessor in place of the constant).

