## What Problem This Solves

Repeated SQLite worker opens can exhaust broker client capacity if request-size calculation throws synchronously. After enough such failures, a valid open can fail with `SQLite worker store capacity reached`.

Closes #202802

## Why This Change Was Made

Catch synchronous errors from input admission at the broker boundary, release the reserved client, and return a rejected error. The input-admission path already releases retained bytes in `finally`; this fix covers the earlier sizing step, which can throw before admission starts. Existing promise rejection cleanup still handles asynchronous failures.

## User Impact

A failed open during request sizing no longer consumes a broker client slot, so later valid SQLite worker work can continue.

## Evidence

- Base `1ed570fb2b288299e2e2a99036d2b9b1e1953a3e`, focused regression command: 64 cyclic selections rejected during sizing, then a valid temporary SQLite open failed with `SQLite worker store capacity reached` (`overloaded`).
- Head `394c4e28c383914137c89d3fcefda21e0c0fdc10`, same focused regression command: passed; 64 invalid opens rejected, then the valid local SQLite append returned `writes: 1`.
- Production-boundary proof `pnpm exec tsx proof-live.ts` called `SqliteWorkerBroker.open()` directly with the same invalid input and a temporary SQLite store. Captured head output:

```text
$ pnpm exec tsx proof-live.ts
{"rejectedOpens":64,"recoveredWriteCount":1}
```

- Canonical precedent: the existing `releases broker client capacity and drains after synchronous cold path failures` broker regression test covers cleanup after synchronous open failures.

