## What Problem This Solves

A scheduled job can receive delivery instructions after OpenClaw has admitted its task. Appending those instructions to the task can change its admission generation before the first tool call.

## User Impact

Scheduled jobs keep their original task and transcript prompt while the runner supplies delivery instructions appropriate to the tools available for that turn.

## Why This Change Was Made

Cron now passes delivery guidance as per-turn runtime context. CLI and embedded execution resolve it once the available tools are known. For plugin-owned transport, the host resolves the guidance conservatively before dispatch. Heartbeat and session-event producers remain open under #172031.

## Evidence

In the embedded run, the admission generation stayed at 3 through the first read and completion. On unpatched main it changed from 3 to 5 before the read. Both CLI runs stayed at generation 4. All four jobs delivered their marker.

Isolated test Gateway on loopback, synthetic data only, one isolated Cron `announce` job per arm (task: read a synthetic marker file and return its line; delivery to a synthetic A2A peer on loopback; job tool allowlist set to `read`; fallbacks disabled). An observer plugin recorded event metadata, including phases, aliased IDs, generations, guidance flags and completion status; `storageGeneration` is the SQLite `transcript_rewrite_watermarks.generation` read at each event. Aliases are per Gateway process. All four arms were valid on the first run.

Exercised revisions: the PR arms ran `d57cb2b4` (base `f3992db5`) and the control arms ran unpatched main `f3992db5`. The current head is `2865137517385c1f763618b3f40dc951207d333e` on integrated main `3d0900e4`. The follow-up to `fe152fc6` changes only a test and its line allocation.

